Ends Android apps the way Android expects. When the activity is destroyed, the cleanup callback runs even though the native window is already gone, by binding the GL context without a surface, and the activity then finishes normally instead of calling exit(), which ran the static destructors of the process while the rendering threads of Android still used them and aborted the process on every close. A new activity in the same process starts from the cleared state, as it already did. sapp_quit() and sapp_request_quit() also work on Android now: a requested quit sends SAPP_EVENTTYPE_QUIT_REQUESTED, which the app may cancel, and a quit calls the cleanup callback and finishes the activity, like the back button.

