+
+    On Android, a programmatic quit calls the cleanup callback and finishes
+    the activity, the same way the back button does. When the activity is
+    destroyed, the cleanup callback is called as well, also when the native
+    window is already gone, by binding the GL context without a surface. The
+    activity then finishes like any other activity: the process isn't
+    terminated with exit(), which would run the static destructors of the
+    process while Android's own rendering threads still use them, and a new
+    activity in the same process starts with a cleared sokol_app state.
 
     On the web platform, the quit behaviour differs from native platforms,
     because of web-specific restrictions:
